DATA={'III/09': {'intro': 'Fourier series decompose periodic functions into orthogonal trigonometric modes. Coefficients are projections, partial sums are finite-dimensional approximations, and convergence depends on both function regularity and the geometry of the trigonometric system.', 'sections': [('Periodic functions', 'A $2\\pi$-periodic function can be viewed as a function on the circle, making trigonometric modes natural coordinates.'), ('Orthogonality', 'Distinct sine and cosine modes are orthogonal on a full period.'), ('Fourier coefficients', 'Coefficients are obtained by integrating against the corresponding orthogonal modes.'), ('Partial sums', 'The $N$th Fourier partial sum is the orthogonal projection onto trigonometric polynomials of degree at most $N$.'), ('Bessel inequality', 'Finite coefficient energy cannot exceed the $L^2$ energy of the function.'), ('Dirichlet kernel', 'Partial sums can be written as convolution with the Dirichlet kernel, making convergence an approximate-identity problem with oscillatory kernels.'), ('Pointwise convergence', 'Piecewise smooth functions converge to their value at continuity points and to midpoint values at jumps under classical Dirichlet hypotheses.'), ('Gibbs phenomenon', 'Near jumps, overshoot persists in relative height while its spatial width shrinks.')], 'theorems': [('Orthogonality relations', 'Distinct trigonometric modes are orthogonal in $L^2[-\\pi,\\pi]$.', 'Use product-to-sum identities; every nonconstant integer-frequency sine or cosine integrates to zero over a full period.'), ('Bessel inequality', 'The square-sum of Fourier coefficients over any finite set of orthonormal modes is bounded by $\\|f\\|_2^2$.', 'Subtract the finite orthogonal projection from $f$ and expand the nonnegative squared norm using orthogonality.'), ('Dirichlet convergence principle', 'Under standard piecewise smooth hypotheses, Fourier partial sums converge at $x$ to $(f(x^-)+f(x^+))/2$.', 'Express the partial sum by the Dirichlet kernel, split the integral into a small neighborhood and its complement, use local one-sided regularity near zero and oscillatory cancellation away from zero.')]}, 'III/10': {'intro': 'Convolution averages one function against translates of another. It interacts cleanly with translation, integration, differentiation, and Fourier transformation, and approximate identities use it to smooth and recover functions.', 'sections': [('Definition of convolution', 'For suitable functions, $(f*g)(x)=\\int f(x-y)g(y)dy$.'), ('Commutativity and associativity', 'Under absolute-integrability hypotheses, changes of variables and Fubini prove $f*g=g*f$ and associativity.'), ('Young inequalities', 'Convolution maps certain $L^p$ pairs into controlled $L^r$ spaces.'), ('Translation viewpoint', 'Convolution is an average of translates of one factor weighted by the other.'), ('Approximate identities', 'Kernels concentrating mass near zero recover functions in norm or pointwise under appropriate hypotheses.'), ('Smoothing', 'Convolution with a smooth compactly supported kernel produces smooth functions.'), ('Differentiation', 'Derivatives pass to the smooth kernel: $D^\\alpha(f*\\phi)=f*D^\\alpha\\phi$ under standard assumptions.'), ('Support', 'For compact supports, $\\operatorname{supp}(f*g)$ lies in the Minkowski sum of the two supports.')], 'theorems': [('Commutativity', 'If the convolution integral is absolutely meaningful, then $f*g=g*f$.', 'Substitute $z=x-y$ in the defining integral.'), ('Young $L^1$ inequality', 'If $f,g\\in L^1(\\mathbb R^n)$, then $f*g\\in L^1$ and $\\|f*g\\|_1\\le\\|f\\|_1\\|g\\|_1$.', 'Use Tonelli on $|f(x-y)g(y)|$ and translation invariance of Lebesgue measure.'), ('Approximate-identity recovery', 'If $\\phi_\\varepsilon$ is an approximate identity and $f\\in L^p$, $1\\le p<\\infty$, then $f*\\phi_\\varepsilon\\to f$ in $L^p$ under standard normalization.', 'Write the difference as an average of translations $\\tau_yf-f$, use continuity of translations in $L^p$, concentrate kernel mass near zero, and control the far tail by kernel mass.')]}, 'III/11': {'intro': 'The Fourier transform converts translation and differentiation into multiplication, convolution into products, and spatial localization into frequency information. It is the central transform chapter of Volume III and is developed with extra structural depth.', 'sections': [('Definition on $L^1$', 'Fix the convention $\\widehat f(\\xi)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^n}f(x)e^{-2\\pi i x\\cdot\\xi}dx$ for integrable functions.'), ('Boundedness and continuity', 'The transform of an $L^1$ function is bounded and uniformly continuous, with $\\|\\widehat f\\|_\\infty\\le\\|f\\|_1$.'), ('Riemann--Lebesgue lemma', 'Fourier transforms of $L^1$ functions vanish at infinity.'), ('Translation and modulation', 'Translation in physical space creates a phase factor in frequency; modulation shifts the transform.'), ('Scaling', 'Dilation in space inversely dilates frequency and contributes the Jacobian factor.'), ('Differentiation and moments', 'Differentiation becomes multiplication by $2\\pi i\\xi$, while multiplication by $x$ becomes differentiation in frequency.'), ('Convolution theorem', 'Convolution transforms into pointwise multiplication, and products transform into convolution when hypotheses permit.'), ('Inversion', 'Under suitable integrability or Schwartz hypotheses, applying the inverse transform reconstructs the original function.'), ('Symmetry and reality', 'Even, odd, real, and radial structures impose corresponding symmetry on the transform.'), ('Uncertainty viewpoint', 'Strong concentration in space conflicts with strong concentration in frequency, foreshadowing quantitative uncertainty principles.')], 'theorems': [('$L^1$ bound', 'For $f\\in L^1$, $|\\widehat f(\\xi)|\\le\\|f\\|_1$ for every $\\xi$.', 'Take absolute values inside the integral; the complex exponential has modulus one.'), ('Riemann--Lebesgue lemma', 'If $f\\in L^1(\\mathbb R^n)$, then $\\widehat f(\\xi)\\to0$ as $|\\xi|\\to\\infty$.', 'First prove it for smooth compactly supported functions by integration by parts or oscillatory cancellation, then approximate an arbitrary $L^1$ function in $L^1$ and use the uniform transform bound.'), ('Convolution theorem', 'If $f,g\\in L^1$, then $\\widehat{f*g}=\\widehat f\\,\\widehat g$.', 'Insert the convolution integral, use Fubini justified by absolute integrability, change variables $u=x-y$, and factor the two resulting integrals.'), ('Fourier inversion on Schwartz space', 'For $f\\in\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^n)$, inverse transformation recovers $f$.', 'Regularize with a Gaussian multiplier, use Fubini to identify the regularized inverse as convolution with an approximate identity, and pass to the limit in the Schwartz or uniform topology.')]}, 'III/12': {'intro': 'The Gaussian is the model function of transform calculus: it is smooth, rapidly decaying, and mapped to another Gaussian. Differentiation, scaling, and tensor-product arguments then generate a large transform table.', 'sections': [('Gaussian integrals', 'The integral of $e^{-\\pi x^2}$ over the line is one under the standard normalization.'), ('Self-reciprocity', 'With the $2\\pi$ Fourier convention, $e^{-\\pi|x|^2}$ is its own Fourier transform.'), ('Scaling', 'Scaled Gaussians transform to reciprocal-width Gaussians with the appropriate determinant factor.'), ('Differentiated Gaussians', 'Polynomial times Gaussian functions arise by differentiating in space or frequency.'), ('Heat kernels', 'Gaussian kernels are the fundamental averaging kernels of the heat equation.'), ('Tensor products', 'The $n$-dimensional transform of a product of one-dimensional Gaussians factors coordinatewise.'), ('Hermite structure', 'Repeated differentiation of Gaussians produces Hermite-polynomial factors and a transform-invariant basis structure.'), ('Transform tables', 'Translation, modulation, scaling, differentiation, and convolution generate many explicit transform pairs from a few seeds.')], 'theorems': [('Gaussian transform', 'Under the convention $\\widehat f(\\xi)=\\int f(x)e^{-2\\pi ix\\xi}dx$, $e^{-\\pi x^2}$ transforms to $e^{-\\pi\\xi^2}$.', 'Differentiate the transform with respect to $\\xi$, integrate by parts to obtain an ODE, solve the ODE, and determine the constant from the Gaussian integral at zero.'), ('Scaling rule', 'If $f_a(x)=f(ax)$ with $a\\ne0$, then $\\widehat{f_a}(\\xi)=|a|^{-1}\\widehat f(\\xi/a)$ in one dimension.', 'Substitute $u=ax$ in the transform integral and account for the Jacobian.'), ('Heat-kernel semigroup', 'Gaussian heat kernels satisfy $G_t*G_s=G_{t+s}$.', 'Fourier transformation turns convolution into multiplication, while $\\widehat G_t$ is an exponential multiplier whose product at times $t,s$ equals the multiplier at $t+s$; invert the transform.')]}, 'III/13': {'intro': 'Plancherel theory extends the Fourier transform from integrable functions to a unitary operator on $L^2$. Orthogonality and energy conservation become the main language, allowing transform methods beyond pointwise integral formulas.', 'sections': [('Parseval identity', 'For suitable functions, the inner product is preserved by the Fourier transform.'), ('Plancherel extension', 'Density of Schwartz functions extends the transform uniquely to all of $L^2$.'), ('Unitarity', 'The $L^2$ transform preserves norm and inner product.'), ('Inverse transform in $L^2$', 'Inversion holds in the Hilbert-space sense even when neither function nor transform is integrable.'), ('Convolution in $L^2$ settings', 'Combining $L^1$ and $L^2$ hypotheses yields useful convolution bounds and multiplier formulas.'), ('Multipliers', 'Bounded frequency multipliers define bounded operators on $L^2$.'), ('Energy methods', 'Differentiation identities translate Sobolev-type derivative energy into weighted frequency energy.'), ('Approximation', 'Frequency cutoffs approximate $L^2$ functions and provide smooth or band-limited regularizations.')], 'theorems': [('Plancherel theorem', 'The Fourier transform extends uniquely to a unitary operator on $L^2(\\mathbb R^n)$.', 'Prove norm preservation on Schwartz functions, use density of Schwartz space in $L^2$ to define the extension by completion, and preserve the norm in the limit.'), ('Parseval identity', 'For $f,g\\in L^2$, $\\langle\\widehat f,\\widehat g\\rangle=\\langle f,g\\rangle$.', 'Use polarization of the norm identity obtained from Plancherel.'), ('$L^2$ multiplier bound', 'If $m\\in L^\\infty$ and $Tf=\\mathcal F^{-1}(m\\widehat f)$, then $\\|Tf\\|_2\\le\\|m\\|_\\infty\\|f\\|_2$.', 'Apply Plancherel and the pointwise bound $|m\\widehat f|\\le\\|m\\|_\\infty|\\widehat f|$.')]}, 'III/14': {'intro': 'The Schwartz space packages smoothness and rapid decay into a Fréchet-type test-function space stable under Fourier transformation. It is the natural common domain for classical transform calculus and tempered distributions.', 'sections': [('Rapid decay', 'A Schwartz function decays faster than every polynomial together with all derivatives.'), ('Schwartz seminorms', 'Quantities $\\sup_x|x^\\alpha D^\\beta f(x)|$ encode the topology.'), ('Algebraic stability', 'Multiplication by polynomials and differentiation preserve Schwartz space.'), ('Translation and modulation', 'Translations and frequency modulations preserve the class.'), ('Fourier invariance', 'The Fourier transform maps Schwartz space bijectively to itself.'), ('Convolution', 'Schwartz functions convolve to Schwartz functions; convolution with appropriate integrable functions smooths while retaining decay.'), ('Density', 'Schwartz space is dense in $L^p$ for finite $p$ and in many Sobolev spaces.'), ('Role in distribution theory', 'Its rapid decay makes pairing with polynomial-growth objects well-defined, leading to tempered distributions.')], 'theorems': [('Fourier invariance', 'If $f\\in\\mathcal S$, then $\\widehat f\\in\\mathcal S$.', 'Differentiation in frequency corresponds to multiplication by powers of $x$, while multiplication by powers of $\\xi$ corresponds to derivatives of $f$; all such weighted derivatives are bounded and integrable for Schwartz functions.'), ('Algebraic stability', 'If $f\\in\\mathcal S$, then $x^\\alpha D^\\beta f\\in\\mathcal S$.', 'Every Schwartz seminorm of this expression is a finite linear combination of higher Schwartz seminorms of $f$.'), ('Schwartz convolution closure', 'If $f,g\\in\\mathcal S$, then $f*g\\in\\mathcal S$.', 'Differentiate under the integral and distribute polynomial weights between $x-y$ and $y$; rapid decay of both factors controls every resulting seminorm.')]}, 'III/15': {'intro': 'Test-function spaces provide compactly supported smooth probes. Their topology is stronger and more local than the Schwartz topology, and distributions are defined as continuous linear functionals on these probes.', 'sections': [('Smooth compact support', '$C_c^\\infty(\\Omega)$ consists of infinitely differentiable functions whose supports are compact subsets of the open set $\\Omega$.'), ('Bump functions', 'Smooth cutoffs allow localization without sharp discontinuities.'), ('Support control', 'A sequence converging in the test-function topology eventually has support inside one fixed compact set.'), ('Derivative control', 'Within that compact set, every derivative converges uniformly.'), ('Partitions of unity', 'Locally finite smooth partitions subordinate to open covers allow local constructions to be patched globally.'), ('Localization', 'Multiplying by a test function isolates distributional behavior in a chosen region.'), ('Comparison with Schwartz space', 'Compact support implies rapid decay after extension by zero, but the topologies and intended duals differ.'), ('Dual space preview', 'The continuous dual of $C_c^\\infty(\\Omega)$ is the space of distributions on $\\Omega$.')], 'theorems': [('Existence of bump functions', 'Given compact $K\\subset U$ with $U$ open in $\\mathbb R^n$, there is $\\phi\\in C_c^\\infty(U)$ with $0\\le\\phi\\le1$ and $\\phi=1$ near $K$.', 'Convolve the indicator of a slightly enlarged neighborhood of $K$ with a sufficiently small smooth mollifier, then truncate the geometric construction so the support stays inside $U$.'), ('Partition of unity', 'Every open cover of a paracompact smooth Euclidean domain admits a smooth locally finite partition of unity subordinate to it.', 'Refine the cover by locally finite relatively compact sets, choose bump functions on the refinement, and normalize their locally finite positive sum.'), ('Test-function convergence', 'If $\\phi_j\\to0$ in $C_c^\\infty(\\Omega)$, then all supports eventually lie in one compact $K\\Subset\\Omega$ and every derivative converges uniformly on $K$.', 'This is the defining sequential behavior of the standard LF topology and is precisely the control needed for distributional continuity.')]}}
